('127.417. d.2.D',3,'A team ranks four stormwater options against cost, maintenance, and space needed. This process is:','["selecting a concept", "naming a pond", "closing a contract", "filing a permit"]'::jsonb,0,'Selection uses criteria.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.D',4,'A decision matrix gives each option a weighted score so the choice rests on the criteria rather than on:','["client needs", "personal preference alone", "project goals", "measured data"]'::jsonb,1,'Matrices add objectivity.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.E',3,'A freehand doodle on a napkin is informal, while a scaled plan sheet with dimensions is a:','["greeting card", "cartoon", "technical drawing", "poster"]'::jsonb,2,'Technical drawings follow standards.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.E',4,'A small physical model of a building is built mainly to help clients and teams:','["hide the cost", "avoid permits", "skip inspection", "visualize the design"]'::jsonb,3,'Models communicate form.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.F',3,'Before pouring a full-size slab, a lab tests small concrete samples to learn how the mix will:','["perform under load", "cost less", "sound when struck", "look at night"]'::jsonb,0,'Tests predict real behavior.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.F',4,'A well-designed experiment changes one factor at a time, such as water content, so the effect of that factor is:','["random", "clear to the tester", "hidden", "meaningless"]'::jsonb,1,'Controlled tests isolate causes.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.G',3,'When a trial pond layout drains too slowly, the team adjusts the outlet size and checks again, showing the process is:','["unscientific", "done once", "repeated as needed", "random"]'::jsonb,2,'Iteration refines the design.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.G',4,'Feedback from reviewers and test data plays a role in iteration by showing the team what to:','["ignore", "hide", "delete", "change next"]'::jsonb,3,'Feedback drives the next version.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.H',3,'Sharing a finished design with the public through clear drawings and plain explanations is effective:','["communication", "secrecy", "delay", "isolation"]'::jsonb,0,'Communication transfers the solution.','original'),
('127.417. d.2.H',4,'Communicating the engineering solution to a contractor with clear drawings reduces the chance of:','["approvals", "misinterpretation", "inspections", "profits"]'::jsonb,1,'Clear documents prevent errors.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.A',3,'A junior engineer lists the week''s tasks in order of due date and importance. This is a time-management strategy called:','["avoiding", "delaying", "prioritizing", "guessing"]'::jsonb,2,'Prioritization manages time.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.A',4,'Setting a deadline two days before the real due date leaves room to handle:','["music", "lunch", "paint", "unexpected problems"]'::jsonb,3,'Buffers absorb surprises.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.B',3,'A project that cannot be built in the rainy season because crews cannot place concrete in the wet faces a:','["weather constraint", "fashion constraint", "paint constraint", "music constraint"]'::jsonb,0,'Constraints come from conditions.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.B',4,'Planning around a constraint such as a utility shutdown window means scheduling the affected work during the:','["last day of the contract", "allowed period", "wrong season", "off hours of nothing"]'::jsonb,1,'Plans fit constraints.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.C',3,'Delivering the soil report on June 1 and the grading plan on July 1 are examples of project:','["rumors", "hobbies", "deliverables and milestones", "vacations"]'::jsonb,2,'Deliverables mark progress.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.C',4,'Dividing a long project into smaller deliverables helps a manager catch trouble:','["never", "later", "after closing", "sooner"]'::jsonb,3,'Short checkpoints reveal problems.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.D',3,'A text message to a coworker asking for a file is informal, while a sealed letter to a client is:','["formal communication", "small talk", "a joke", "a rumor"]'::jsonb,0,'Formality varies with purpose.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.D',4,'Choosing a phone call over email for an urgent site issue works because it provides:','["a permanent record only", "immediate feedback", "less clarity", "a lower cost only"]'::jsonb,1,'Mode affects speed.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.E',3,'When a pipe will not fit, a team lists possible causes and tests them one at a time, using the strategy of:','["giving up", "random guessing", "systematic troubleshooting", "wishful thinking"]'::jsonb,2,'Systematic methods solve problems.','original'),
('127.417. d.3.E',4,'Problem solving works better when a team shares what each member knows because it widens the set of:','["excuses", "delays", "problems", "possible solutions"]'::jsonb,3,'Collaboration adds ideas.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.A',3,'Under federal law, a contractor must give crews training, protective gear, and a worksite with no known serious:','["hazards", "paychecks", "schedules", "breaks"]'::jsonb,0,'Employers must provide safe work.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.A',4,'A worker who sees a damaged scaffold plank has a responsibility to avoid using it and to:','["hide it", "report it", "paint it", "remove the sign"]'::jsonb,1,'Workers share safety duties.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.B',3,'A federal agency that sets and enforces workplace safety rules, including those for trenching, is:','["NASA", "NOAA", "OSHA", "FEMA"]'::jsonb,2,'OSHA regulates workplace safety.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.B',4,'OSHA requires a trench deeper than five feet to have a protective system because cave-ins can cause:','["faster work", "lower pay", "extra shade", "fatal injuries"]'::jsonb,3,'Trenching is a leading cause of death.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.C',3,'An industrial hygienist measuring silica dust at a saw-cutting site is trying to prevent long-term:','["lung disease", "slow traffic", "bad grammar", "low pay"]'::jsonb,0,'Hygiene protects health from exposures.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.C',4,'The hierarchy of controls says the best way to handle a hazard is to first try to:','["post a sign", "eliminate it", "ignore it", "wear a mask"]'::jsonb,1,'Elimination beats PPE.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.D',3,'A welder needs a face shield, while a concrete finisher needs waterproof boots. Matching gear to the hazard is the proper use of:','["site rumors", "office supplies", "personal protective equipment", "paint samples"]'::jsonb,2,'PPE depends on the task.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.D',4,'Hearing protection is required on a site when noise levels reach a point that could cause permanent:','["sleepiness", "good mood", "boredom", "hearing loss"]'::jsonb,3,'Noise exposure damages ears.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.E',3,'Keeping walkways clear of tools and debris reduces the chance that a worker will suffer a:','["trip and fall", "paper cut", "bad review", "sunburn"]'::jsonb,0,'Housekeeping prevents falls.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.E',4,'Workers on a roof edge must use a guardrail system or a personal fall arrest system once the drop reaches:','["six inches", "six feet", "sixty feet", "six miles"]'::jsonb,1,'Construction fall protection begins at six feet.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.F',3,'Using a power tool with a missing ground pin can send current through the user, which is an electrical:','["scheduling hazard", "color hazard", "shock hazard", "noise hazard"]'::jsonb,2,'Grounding prevents shock.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.F',4,'Overhead power lines near a crane boom create a risk of:','["fashion issues", "rain delay", "boredom", "electrocution"]'::jsonb,3,'Cranes must stay clear of lines.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.G',3,'A worker needs to know the fire and health risks of a solvent before opening it, so she reads the:','["safety data sheet", "menu", "timesheet", "gossip column"]'::jsonb,0,'SDSs describe chemical hazards.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.G',4,'Compressed gas cylinders should be stored upright and secured so they cannot fall and:','["look neat", "break their valves", "cost less", "get lost"]'::jsonb,1,'Falling cylinders can become projectiles.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.H',3,'A map posted at the trailer showing the assembly point, first aid kit, and fire extinguishers is a worksite safety:','["menu", "puzzle", "resource", "joke"]'::jsonb,2,'Resources prepare workers.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.H',4,'Knowing where the nearest eyewash station is matters because chemical splashes in the eye require treatment within:','["weeks", "years", "months", "seconds"]'::jsonb,3,'Quick flushing reduces damage.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.I',3,'A system in which managers commit resources and workers help find hazards is the core of a written safety and health:','["program", "hiring rule", "marketing plan", "parking policy"]'::jsonb,0,'Programs organize safety.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.I',4,'A written program should include regular worksite inspections so hazards can be found and:','["photographed only", "corrected", "admired", "copied"]'::jsonb,1,'Inspections find problems to fix.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.J',3,'A written emergency plan names who calls 911, where crews gather, and how to account for everyone after an:','["vacation", "election", "evacuation", "audit"]'::jsonb,2,'Plans give specific roles.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.J',4,'Practicing the evacuation plan twice a year helps workers stay calm and move quickly when there is a real:','["holiday", "award", "lunch", "emergency"]'::jsonb,3,'Drills build readiness.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.K',3,'A hazard communication program requires that containers be labeled and that workers learn how to read the:','["labels and data sheets", "contracts", "traffic signs", "menu boards"]'::jsonb,0,'HazCom tells workers what they handle.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.K',4,'A skull-and-crossbones pictogram on a chemical label signals:','["a bonus", "acute toxicity", "a bargain", "a bakery"]'::jsonb,1,'Pictograms quickly show hazard type.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.L',3,'Safety rules say a new worker must be trained on the hazards of a task and the controls for them before starting the:','["lunch", "review", "job", "vacation"]'::jsonb,2,'Training precedes exposure.','original'),
('127.417. d.4.L',4,'A refresher course on fall protection every year helps workers remember what they have learned and update their:','["wages", "uniforms", "tools", "knowledge"]'::jsonb,3,'Retraining maintains skills.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.A',3,'A project engineer lists the tasks for a site design and ranks them so the ones that block other work are done:','["first", "at random", "last", "never"]'::jsonb,0,'Priorities follow dependencies.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.A',4,'Ranking tasks by their impact on the schedule helps the engineer protect the project''s:','["lobby", "deadline", "logo", "parking"]'::jsonb,1,'Priority protects the finish date.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.B',3,'Tasks A (3 days), B (5 days), and C (2 days) must be done one after another. The critical path length is:','["15 days", "5 days", "10 days", "3 days"]'::jsonb,2,'3 + 5 + 2 = 10.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.B',4,'If task D can run alongside B and takes 2 days, D is not critical because B already takes:','["the same pay", "fewer people", "less time", "longer"]'::jsonb,3,'The longer parallel task controls.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.C',3,'A budget based on billable hours multiplies the hours estimated for each task by the engineer''s:','["hourly rate", "birth date", "parking space", "age"]'::jsonb,0,'Fee = hours x rate.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.C',4,'A task estimated at 12 hours at 85 dollars per hour costs:','["10,200 dollars", "1,020 dollars", "102 dollars", "97 dollars"]'::jsonb,1,'12 x 85 = 1,020.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.D',3,'Engineers record the hours they spend on each task on a timesheet so the firm can bill the client and see how the work compares with the:','["song list", "weather", "budget", "menu"]'::jsonb,2,'Timekeeping tracks cost.','original'),
('127.417. d.5.D',4,'Time tracked at 30 hours against a 24 hour estimate shows the task has gone:','["exactly on budget", "under budget", "unbilled", "over budget"]'::jsonb,3,'Overruns signal a problem.','original'),
